WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Since 2024, the central quad has been watered by a controller marketed as occupancy-aware.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.590
When the lawn is busy, a sensor should shorten the watering, or skip it.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.270
It had never been checked independently.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.110
A paper from the School of Continuous Improvement asks:

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.360
does it respond to the crowd, or just repeat its timetable?

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.600
We put two infrared counters on each of the quad’s four zones,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.950
and checked them by hand every fortnight.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.640
For seven weeks, they counted the crowd before each watering,

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.280
and measured how much of it the controller held back.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.090
Across eighty-four sessions, the correlation was point zero four.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.870
Turf, the busiest zone, sits on the same flat line as the quietest.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.880
Then, in week eight, facilities staff unplugged the sensor.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.150
For two weeks, the controller ran on its fixed timetable alone.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.850
In every zone, the watering barely moved.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.660
Turf got a hundred and fifty-five litres a session with the sensor,

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.750
and a hundred and fifty-six without it.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:04.062
The interesting number isn’t the correlation, it’s the two per cent.

00:01:04.062 --> 00:01:07.102
That’s how little changed when we took the sensor out of the loop.

00:01:08.002 --> 00:01:14.022
The system is, on this evidence, running its original fixed timetable under an occupancy-aware label.

00:01:14.322 --> 00:01:17.202
A smart label is often a promise, not a demonstrated property.

00:01:18.102 --> 00:01:22.862
Every occupancy-aware system we operate is going to need this check eventually.

00:01:23.162 --> 00:01:26.752
We’d rather run it ourselves, on our own lawn, than wait for someone else to ask.
